  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nSurface high pressure currently centered over eastern Ontario \nwill keep conditions dry across our area today. Sunshine is \nexpected to be dimmed by mid and high level clouds that are \nstreaming in from the west associated with the system that will\nbe bringing our next chance for rain later tonight into Sunday.\nClouds are expected to thicken later in the day as high \npressure slides off to the east. After a cool start early this \nmorning with temperatures primarily in the 40s and low 50s, high\ntemperatures this afternoon will be a few degrees below \naverage, but overall it will be a fairly comfortable day with \nlight winds and low humidity as highs range from the mid 60s to \nthe low 70s.\n \nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA low pressure system will approach from the west later tonight,\npotentially kicking off some isolated to scattered showers \nahead of it. Rain will become more widespread early Sunday \nmorning and continues throughout the day as the low moves across\nthe region. The track of this low has trended a bit farther \nnorth over the past 24 hours, so there is still a little bit of \nuncertainty there. Most guidance was lower than NBM with regards\nto high temperatures Sunday, so they were lowered about 2 to 4 \ndegrees, ranging from the upper 50s to the mid 60s. \n\nThe NAM does show around 500-1000 J/kg of MUCAPE across parts \nof NE PA, mainly over Luzerne, Lackawanna and Wyoming Counties,\nSunday and there is 30-40 knots of shear, but lapse rates are \nlow, so just a couple of rumbles seem possible. Thunder chances \nwere removed completely north of the NY/PA border. \n\nWPC continues to highlight from around the NYS Thruway on south\nin a marginal risk for excessive rainfall Sunday. Looking at \nguidance, PWATs peak mainly between 1.50 and 1.80 inches (but \ncloser to 2 inches in some spots on the Euro) and warm cloud \ndepths will near 12k feet. There is also increasing winds aloft,\nmost notably around 850mb that could enhance lift. All of this \ncan lead to some periods of locally heavy rainfall. Guidance is \nstill spread on exactly where the highest rainfall totals will \nbe, though the general focus seems to be across the Twin Tiers, \nNE PA and into the Catskills with a widespread 1 to 1.50 inches\nof rain, with localized higher amounts, through Monday. Looking\nat the latest flash flood guidance, both 3 hour county and \nheadwater flash flood guidance is about 2 to 2.5 inches of rain,\nexcept toward the Poconos where it is higher. Soil moistures \nare also abnormally dry across north-central NY, so confidence \nis low for any flash flood issues through some ponding in urban \nand poor drainage areas will be possible.\n\nThis low moves east of the region late Sunday night and drags a\nfront south of the region. Behind this system, high pressure \nbrings drier air back in for Monday. Being in zonal flow, some \npassing showers will be possible across portions of NE PA and \nthe Catskills. However, the latest model runs are a bit farther \nsouth with the placement of the high, which then deflects most \nof the moisture to our south, so shower chances have trended \nlower. Broad surface high pressure centered of eastern Canada \nwill then compete with additional disturbances Tuesday through \nThursday. An upper low will develop over the Great Lakes region \nearly in the week and still under zonal flow, showers will \ncontinue to move through the region. Just how far north these \nshowers will be is uncertain with the high pressure to the \nnorth. The upper low will move little by Wednesday. East to \nsouthwesterly flow will advect Atlantic moisture inland, \nsupporting showers midweek. The upper low becomes an open wave \nas a surface low develops over the Mid-Atlantic coast. This low \nwill slide up the coast with the next round of showers on \nThursday.\n\nThe first extended taste of fall is expected next week. \nTemperatures will only max out in the 50s and 60s before falling\ninto the 30s and 40s at night. Fall officially begins this \nTuesday at 804 PM EDT.",
